+# Audit 2026-09-04 — findings, fixes applied, and what is still open
+
+Read this together with `docs/HANDOFF_CLAUDE_CODE.md`. That file describes the
+contract; this file describes what was found broken against it on commit
+`6ff8992`, which of it is fixed in this change set, and what the next engineer
+should do first. Every fix is marked in code with a comment starting
+`AUDIT 2026-09-04 #<n>` where `<n>` is the finding number below, and every fix
+has a regression test in `tests/test_audit_2026_09_regressions.py`.
+
+How the audit was run: first on a synthetic `data/1.1 Configurator.xlsx`
+(all catalog rows, fake prices), then re-run on the real workbook supplied by
+the owner (52 sheets, 5333 price observations, 1036 priced options). Everything
+below marked "real workbook" was reproduced on real prices. The full pytest
+suite and a 45-RFQ contract battery were run on the real workbook; results are
+in the "Verification" section.
+
+## Fixed in this change set
+
+### #1 Non-English text produced a silently reduced, approvable plan — FIXED
+
+`Нужен сервер Dell R760, 2 процессора Xeon 6430, 256 ГБ ОЗУ, 4 диска 1.92 ТБ SSD`
+returned `COMPLETE`, `ready=True`: DIRECT had 1 CPU, 2x32 GB, no drives, no
+deviation. The parser only knows English units and quantities; completion
+filled the rest with defaults.
+
+Fix: `new_path/service.py` — `contains_non_english()` (any Cyrillic letter)
+short-circuits `_run` with `NON_ENGLISH_INPUT`; copy in `presentation/copy.py`.
+Applies to first messages and to clarification answers.
+
+Still open: the same failure mode exists for English text whose numbers the
+parser does not understand (see #10, #11). A general "unparsed number with unit"
+guard is the right next step: compare numbers+units found by a broad regex
+against `ParseResult.extracted_fields` and ask instead of quoting when something
+material was ignored.
+
+### #2 Short clarification answers restarted the request — FIXED
+
+`looks_like_full_rfq()` returned True for `Dell R760`, `256GB`, `64 GB`,
+`Xeon Gold 6430`. `handle_clarification_answer` calls it before
+`apply_clarification`, so answering a question with the thing the bot asked for
+discarded the original RFQ. Now at least two independent specification signals
+are required (model, CPU, RAM, storage, network, GPU, workload), or a long
+workload description. `test_new_path_sticky_state_full_rfq_wins` still passes.
+
+### #3 The planner blocked the Telegram event loop for 7–19 s — FIXED
+
+Measured `process_text` on the synthetic workbook: 7.4 s, 8.1 s, 15.6 s,
+18.9 s. Every other manager, and `/stop`, waited. `_plan_request` runs the
+planner twice when the first pass is not COMPLETE, and `apply_manual_price`
+rebuilds the planner and re-reads the workbook on every confirmed price.
+
+Fix: `bot/handlers.py` runs `process_text`, `apply_clarification`,
+`apply_manual_price` and `approve` through `asyncio.to_thread`;
+`NewPathCPQService` serialises its public entry points with an `RLock`
+because it holds mutable state (`_sessions`, planner rebuild). Throughput is
+unchanged (one request at a time); responsiveness is fixed.
+
+Still open: cache the parsed workbook across planner rebuilds; make the
+planner a single pass with both pending modes.
+
+### #4 Price entry mis-parsed and poisoned the price journal — FIXED
+
+`_parse_price("1,250")` returned 1.25; `1,250.00` and `1.250,00` too. `$1 200`
+was read as SKU `1` price 200. A new RFQ typed while a price was pending
+(`Dell R760 8SFF, 2x Xeon 6430, 256GB RAM`) was read as price 7608 and offered
+for confirmation. After `yes`, `journal.set_catalog_price` stores it by display
+name, and `ManagerPurchaseDbSource` is the first price source, so the wrong
+value outranks the workbook for every later quote. There is no undo.
+
+Fix: `_normalize_number` handles thousands/decimal separators; `_parse_price`
+accepts only price-shaped messages; `_parse_sku_and_price` requires a SKU token
+containing a letter; `handle_manual_price` applies the same `looks_like_full_rfq`
+guard as the clarification handler and starts a fresh request instead.
+
+Still open: a `/prices undo` or expiry for manual prices, and `/sources`
+showing who entered a manual price and when.
+
+### #5 25GbE SFP28 could not be built anywhere — FIXED (parser) / PARTLY (catalog)
+
+`parsing/extractors/network.py` alternation `sfp\+?|sfp28` matched `sfp` inside
+`sfp28`, so media became `SFP+` and `nic_media_matches()` rejected every SFP28
+card: `... 2x 25GbE SFP28` gave `engineering_candidates=0` on all 45 platforms,
+Dell and HPE alike. Reordered to `sfp28|sfp\+?`.
+
+After the fix HPE Gen11 builds DIRECT with 25GbE (real workbook: DL380 Gen11 +
+Broadcom BCM57414 OCP3, DEGRADED because no ALTERNATIVE/VALUE has 25G). Dell
+NEW platforms still have no 25 GbE card at all: the four NEW 25G cards are HPE
+part numbers and OEM-locked in `compatibility_curation.yaml`; the only Dell
+25G row in the committed catalog (`Dell Mellanox ConnectX-4 CX422A Lx 2x25G
+SFP28 NDC`) is no longer present in the current workbook, and the generic
+`Mellanox ConnectX-4 Lx NIC (Dual-Port 25GbE SFP28)` has no form factor in its
+label. A label-regex change that would have read "2x25G" was tried and
+reverted: on the current workbook its only effect was to give the QLogic
+QLE2692 16G FC HBA a NIC speed. Fixing Dell 25GbE needs a real catalog row
+(workbook line or `component_attribute_overrides.yaml` with provenance), not
+parser work.
+
+Note that `compatibility_relations.yaml` contains 0 of 61 NICs; NIC
+compatibility is form-factor only (`nic_forms` per family in
+`compatibility_curation.yaml`) and never checks that a platform actually has a
+free OCP3/NDC slot.
+
+### #6 Missing workbook crashed the bot at startup with a traceback — FIXED (message only)
+
+`create_dispatcher` builds `NewPathCPQService` unconditionally; its price
+repository loads the workbook eagerly, so a missing/mistyped
+`SERVERMALL_EXCEL_PATH` killed the process regardless of `CPQ_NEW_PATH_ENABLED`.
+`main.run_bot` now exits with a clear message. The eager load itself is
+unchanged.
+
+Also related: CI (`.github/workflows/test.yml`) restores the workbook from a
+secret; without it `test_e2_planner`, `test_e25_new_platforms`, `test_f2_e2e`,
+`test_f1_completion_e2e`, `test_mvp_acceptance_corpus` and others fail rather
+than skip. Ruff is not run in CI.
+
+### #24 Price prompts said USD while pricing is EUR end to end — FIXED
+
+Commit `fe179f6` made every price source EUR (`ManagerPurchaseDbSource`,
+`ConfiguratorPriceSource`, `PriceRepository.base_currency`). The `/prices`
+prompts, the confirmation text and the range error still said USD, so a manager
+following the prompt typed a dollar figure that was stored and marked up as
+euro (about 8% low at today's rate) and later shown in `/sources` as
+"12.50 EUR purchase; manager-confirmed". Prompts now say EUR ex VAT.
+`FX_USD_EUR` in `.env.example`/`config.py` and handoff §5 ("treated as USD")
+are stale and should be removed or re-documented.
+
+### #27–#30 Prose RFQs lost CPUs, drives, RAM and network — FIXED (real workbook)
+
+The realistic e-mail `Hi, we need a quote for a Dell PowerEdge R760 with two
+Intel Xeon Gold 6430 processors, 512 GB of DDR5 memory, eight 1.92 TB SAS SSDs
+in RAID 10, dual 25 GbE SFP28 ports, redundant power supplies and rail kit`
+produced a COMPLETE, approvable plan with 1 CPU, 2x 1.2 TB HDD and no NIC.
+Four independent parser gaps, all fixed with regression tests:
+
+- #27 `parsing/normalize.py`: number words and `dual` before a component or a
+  number (`two Intel Xeon`, `eight 1.92 TB`, `dual 25 GbE`), `N processors`,
+  `dual-socket`, and terabyte memory (`1 TB RAM`, `1TB DDR5`, `1.5TB of
+  memory`) all parsed to nothing. `1TB RAM` silently became the 64 GB default.
+- #28 `parsing/extractors/network.py`: `2 ports 25GbE SFP28` matched two
+  patterns and produced two requirements (4 ports).
+- #29 `parsing/extractors/storage.py`: `40TB usable RAID6 SAS HDD` lost media
+  and protocol because they followed the RAID level; the planner then quoted
+  5x 15.36 TB SAS SSD (EUR 38k) instead of HDDs (EUR 12k).
+- #30 `parsing/extractors/storage.py`: a fixed 24-character look-ahead used to
+  reject a drive pack as "network-looking" whenever the next clause contained
+  `GbE`, so `8x 1.92 TB SAS SSDs, 2x 25 GbE` lost the drives. The window now
+  stops at the clause boundary.
+
+Still open: a generic "material number not consumed by any extractor" guard,
+so the next unknown phrasing asks instead of quoting defaults.
+
+### #16 Docker build was broken — FIXED
+
+`Dockerfile` copied a `templates` directory that does not exist. Removed, plus
+the matching compose volume and `TEMPLATES_DIR`. Production runs under systemd;
+the Docker path is untested beyond building.
+
+### #14 (partial) Stale user-facing copy — FIXED
+
+`/start` and `/quote` described "Exact / Alternative / REF" and PDF/DOCX/XLSX
+upload. Both now describe plain-text input and DIRECT / ALTERNATIVE / VALUE.
+
+## Found, not fixed — ordered by impact
+
+### #7 NEW quotes contain REF-labelled drives without a deviation (real workbook)
+
+In the workbook 60 of 61 `HDD / SSD` rows carry no NEW/REF marker, and the
+catalog builder records them all as `condition: REF`. A NEW DIRECT for
+`4x 1.92TB SAS SSD` therefore uses `cmp_storage_1920_0_sas_sff_4b05479fc7`
+(REF, EUR 1360 each) with no deviation, and the same row at the same price is
+the VALUE (REF) drive. Either the drives are new and the condition inference is
+wrong, or a NEW quote is shipping refurbished media undisclosed. Decide with
+the business owner and either tag conditions in the workbook or add a
+`ref_component_in_new_build` deviation in `d1/builder.py`.
+
+### #8 Dell NEW builds never carry tray caddies (real workbook, systematic)
+
+In the 45-RFQ battery every Dell NEW platform with drives (R760, R760xs,
+R7615, R7625) has 0 tray lines while HPE NEW and all REF builds have one tray
+per drive: 13 of 23 COMPLETE plans are affected. `d1/builder.py` adds trays
+only when `get_compatible_trays` returns something, and the catalog has no
+Gen15/Gen16 Dell tray with a price, so the absence is silent. Handoff §6
+describes the platform-scoped `pending:tray:...` line for *explicit* tray
+requests; the same mechanism should fire for implicit trays so the BOM is
+either complete or visibly PRICE_PENDING.
+
+### #31 DIMM population ignores sockets (real workbook)
+
+`384GB` on two CPUs is built as 3x 128 GB, `192GB` as 3x 128 GB — an odd DIMM
+count across two sockets (one CPU gets one DIMM) on R740, DL380 Gen10, R640,
+R760 and DL360 Gen11 alike. The RAM chooser minimises DIMM count and price with
+no per-socket balance or channel rule. Add: DIMM count divisible by CPU count,
+prefer equal channels per socket, and disclose when the requested total is not
+reachable in a balanced layout.
+
+### #32 Pathological planning time on simple requests (real workbook)
+
+`small server for Active Directory and file sharing, 32GB RAM, 2x 2TB SATA
+RAID1` took 408 s (with the test suite competing for CPU; other requests took
+2–70 s under the same load). T560 took 71 s, T360 53 s. Requests with few hard
+constraints enumerate hundreds of engineering candidates (`t560:
+engineering_candidates=100`) and each candidate is priced and validated. With
+#3 the bot stays responsive, but a manager still waits minutes. Profile
+`planning/planner.py` candidate generation and add an early cut-off by price
+rank per role.
+
+### #33 Coverage gaps on current mainstream platforms (real workbook)
+
+From the battery: `Dell R660` (22 priced candidates, `post_diversity=0`),
+`Dell R650`, `Dell R7525` (0 engineering candidates), `Dell T560`, `R760 +
+NVMe RAID10`, `R760 + two pools`, `2TB DDR5`, `3x drives RAID1` (should ask,
+not gap), `database 2x 6448Y + 1TB + NVMe` (DEGRADED), `virtualization 32
+cores/socket + 1TB + 10TB NVMe + 4x 25GbE` (DEGRADED). 19 of 45 realistic
+requests end in CATALOG_COVERAGE_GAP. `HPE DL385 Gen11` is answered with a
+Dell R7625 DIRECT (disclosed). `file server 100TB usable` gets an ALTERNATIVE
+of 9x 15.36 TB SSD at EUR 59k against a EUR 14k DIRECT: ALTERNATIVE has no
+price-band check relative to DIRECT.
+
+### #9 NVMe RAID10 on R760 8SFF is reported as a catalog gap
+
+`Dell R760 8SFF NEW, 2x Xeon Gold 6430, 256GB DDR5, 4x 1.92TB NVMe RAID10`:
+`fully_priced_candidates=4, post_diversity_candidates=0` → NO_VALID_OPTIONS.
+Four built and priced candidates are discarded by the diversity filter in
+`planning/planner.py`. The filter should degrade to two roles (DEGRADED), not to
+nothing. Reproduce with the probe in the audit report.
+
+### #25 The committed catalog is behind the workbook (real workbook)
+
+`test_catalog_attrs_matches_the_workbook` on the current workbook: 50 rows new
+in the workbook, 12 gone, 123 parsed differently. New rows include current CPUs
+(Gold 6438Y+ RENEW, Gold 6244, EPYC 9965) and junk cells from manager sheets
+(`???`, `Ву`, `6230.0`, `8268.0`); gone rows include the Dell 25G NDC and the
+Broadcom P2100G. Two manager sheets write a numeric `Position` (`15.0`,
+`25.0`). Regenerating with `scripts/build_catalog_attrs.py` +
+`build_normalized_catalog.py` changes three resource files by ~2000 lines and
+must be followed by `build_compatibility_relations.py` and the audit-artifact
+scripts (handoff §16); it was not done in this change set because it is a
+catalog release, not a bug fix. Until then the bot quotes from a catalog that
+does not match the price book it reads.
+
+### #26 An FC HBA is a "NIC" (real workbook)
+
+`HPE SN1100Q 2x16GB FC Pcie` is category `nic` with `speed_gb: 16`, no media.
+`... 2 ports 16Gb/s` selects it as the network adapter on DL380 Gen10, R740 and
+R640 (an HPE HBA in Dell chassis). Any media-less request with speed 11–16
+would do the same. Give FC/HBA rows their own category or exclude them from
+`get_compatible_nics`.
+
+### #10 Workload defaults produce commercially implausible CPUs
+
+`Need a virtualization server, 512GB RAM, 20TB usable SSD, 2 CPUs` → DIRECT
+2x Xeon Bronze 3408U (8C), VALUE 2x Xeon Silver 4112 (4C). The virtualization
+profile in `resources/workload_profiles.yaml` sets `default_min_cpu_cores=8`
+per server and the planner takes the cheapest CPU above the floor. Add
+per-workload cores-per-socket and minimum tier floors.
+
+### #11 Quoting with no requirements
+
+`need a server` → question → `I don't know` → full COMPLETE plan on R760xs. A
+non-answer to the only critical question should block, not proceed with
+defaults. See `clarification/policy.py` handling of an unrecognised answer.
+
+### #12 Production host, root user and password SSH in a public repo
+
+`deploy/deploy.py:21-22` hard-codes `HOST`, `USER = "root"`, password from
+`DEPLOY_SSH_PASSWORD`, `paramiko.AutoAddPolicy()`; same in 11 other scripts
+under `deploy/` and `scripts/`. No token or password was found in the tree or
+in the last 50 commits, but the handoff says credentials were shared in the
+project history. Move host/user to env, switch to key auth, pin the host key,
+disable root password login, rotate the password.
+
+### #13 FSM state is in-memory
+
+`Dispatcher()` uses MemoryStorage; `request_id`, `pending_prices` and price
+confirmations vanish on every restart/deploy. New-path sessions are persisted
+in the journal, so a SQLite/Redis FSM storage (or re-attaching the last draft
+session per chat on `/prices` and `/approve`) would close the gap.
+
+### #14 Production flag defaults off; README describes the legacy product
+
+`CPQ_NEW_PATH_ENABLED` defaults to false in `config.py` and `.env.example`
+while the handoff calls the new path production. A fresh environment or a lost
+`.env` silently runs `simple/` (files, OCR, different logic). Flip the default
+or refuse to start without an explicit value; rewrite README.
+
+### #15 `APP_ENV` defaults to development, which opens the bot to everyone
+
+`bot/access.py`: an empty allowlist admits all users unless `APP_ENV` is
+`production`/`prod`. `/sources` exposes purchase prices. Consider making the
+allowlist mandatory whenever a bot token is set, and not replying to
+unauthorised users at all.
+
+### #17–#23 Smaller items
+
+- `_safe_process` sends raw exception text to the chat.
+- `FX_USD_EUR=0.92` is a fixed env value with no date; manual prices are USD,
+  display is EUR.
+- Catalog labels contain Cyrillic letters (`Caсhe` in Xeon Silver 4112) and
+  prices (`... 1440eur`); `T160` is categorised as an accessory from an
+  `Extra 1` row, which is why the planner sees no T160.
+- CI runs only on `main`, no Ruff, full suite ≈ 3 h.
+- `tests/test_channel_rfqs.py:21` contains a real-looking email at a real
+  domain; confirm it is fictitious.
+- `simple/`, `shadow/`, `extraction/`, `models/` and the pdf/docx/ocr
+  dependencies are legacy-only.
+- Logs carry no `user_id`/`request_id` correlation.
+
+## Verification of this change set (real workbook)
+
+- `ruff check src tests scripts`: clean.
+- `tests/test_audit_2026_09_regressions.py`: 58 tests, all pass, no workbook
+  needed (parser, price parsing, guards, and aiogram handler flows through a
+  real FSM).
+- Parser suites on the patched code (`test_f2_parser`, `test_f2_completion`,
+  `test_f25_real_rfq`, `test_f3_clarification`, `test_f1_completion`,
+  `test_f16_reused_hardware`): 564 pass.
+- Full suite with the real workbook, `pytest tests -n 4`: **1854 passed,
+  14 failed in 50 min**. The same 13 tests fail identically on the unpatched
+  `6ff8992` with the same workbook (re-run in a separate worktree), so none is a
+  regression of this change set; the 14th (`test_f35_shadow::
+  test_shadow_not_hooked_on_photo_or_documents`) failed only because
+  `handlers.py` was being edited while the suite ran and passes on both trees.
+  The 13 pre-existing failures:
+  - `test_catalog_attrs` (2): the committed catalog is behind the workbook (#25);
+  - `test_commercial_rfq_acceptance[live-t360-raid1-ssd]`: known open, handoff §10.2;
+  - `test_commercial_rfq_acceptance[named-t360-192|256]`,
+    `test_unified_engine[wa_t360|call_perc_not_gpu]`, `test_simple_config` (2),
+    `test_e15_price_coverage::test_priced_ram_layout_resolves_128gb_module_blocker`:
+    price/catalog outcomes that moved with the newer workbook; re-check after the
+    catalog is regenerated;
+  - `test_e25_new_platforms`, `test_e26_dell_new_coverage`,
+    `test_compatibility_audit_artifact`: generated audit artifacts are no longer
+    byte-identical to the current workbook/code; regenerate with their scripts.
+- 45-RFQ contract battery (real workbook, before the parser fixes): 23
+  COMPLETE, 10 NO_VALID_OPTIONS, 9 DEGRADED, 3 blocked as unsupported. The
+  probe script and results are in the audit report outside this repository;
+  the systematic findings are #8, #31, #32, #33 above.
+- Manual price lifecycle on a real SQLite journal (R760 + Tray Caddy):
+  PRICE_PENDING → `/approve` blocked → `0W4CN 12.50` → COMPLETE → repeated RFQ
+  reuses the price. Works.
